  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1050 PM MST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nThe H500 hand analysis map this evening is playing out as\nexpected with an Omega-like pattern in place. A strong \ndownstream blocking trough over eastern NOAM is forcing the \nupstream trough to negatively tilt as it moves farther inland. \nIn between the highly amplified ridge remains but has shifted \nonto the Plains from 24 hours ago. Showers have developed in the\nwestern CWA and with a strong PV lobe rotating across the 4 \nCorners overnight...a few strikes of lightning will be possible.\nWeaker echos on the radar suggest moisture is increasing on the\nsoutherly flow into the southern mountains but so far webcams \nand snotels are relatively quiet. This will be changing in the \nnext few hours with the heaviest precipitation still expected \nduring the early to mid morning hours on Saturday as the surface\nfront and large scale coincide with moisture in place. Snow \nlevels still look to hover in the 8-9kft level for the bulk of \nthis event...lowering to mountain bases by sunset tomorrow as \nthe system is exiting. The southern mountains...especially the \ncrescent of the southern San Juans remain favored to catch the \nbest moisture and produce the highest snow amounts. 4 to 8 \ninches should cover most of the advisory area though hi-res \nguidance is picking out some of the favored high peaks and \nsnotels for the higher amounts closer to a foot. Otherwise 1 to \n4 inches should cover the remaining mountain forecasts though \ncolder northwest flow often throws some bonus inches. QPF \namounts in the southern basins are still in the 1/3 to 2/3 inch \nrange for liquid precipitation with again some isolated higher \namounts possible due to the convection. Hydrographs are still \nonly showing some minor rises on the smaller tributaries to the \nSan Juan with the main steam holding steady. Temperatures will \nbe much cooler tomorrow in the post frontal environment but \noverall end up near to above normal in the drier northern CWA \nand 5 to 10 degrees cooler in the wetter southern areas. By \nsunrise on Sunday the trough and PV stream will be east of the \ndivide and ridging will be translate across the Great Basin to \nthe Great Plains through Monday afternoon as the next deep \ntrough arrives to the West. Models still show a moderate AR with\nthis system which again survives the upstream terrain and \nbrings more widespread precipitation to the area in the mid week\ntime frame. The QG forcing has been trending a bit farther \nNorth which will bring better chances for precipitation to our \nnorthern CWA which as been missing out lately. This system is a \ntad cooler and this should translate to better a snowfall \nforecast for most of the higher terrain...but will need to \ncontinue to watch trends in speed and track as we get nearer. \nTemperatures will moderate to near normal Monday into Tuesday \nbefore a colder end to the week. Models spread still significant\nand changing daily on how the pattern in the late week period \nevolves but the overall flavor is for a large trough in the West\nkeeping unsettled conditions on the radar.",
